---
// one real answer to a question people type into google. rules of thumb,
// labelled as such. plan sizes come off the yaml so the table can't drift.
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { price } from "../../lib/order";

const fivem = await getEntry("games", "fivem");
const plans = [...fivem!.data.plans].sort((a, b) => a.pricing.monthly - b.pricing.monthly);

const siteUrl = "https://lumixsolutions.org";
const published = "2026-11-25";
const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "How Much RAM and CPU Does a FiveM Server Need?",
  description:
    "What a FiveM server really needs in CPU, RAM and storage, by player count and framework, and how to avoid the asset mistakes that make players stall on the loading screen.",
  datePublished: published,
  dateModified: published,
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Lumix Solutions" },
  publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Lumix Solutions", logo: { "@type": "ImageObject", url: `${siteUrl}/icon-512.png` } },
  mainEntityOfPage: `${siteUrl}/guides/fivem-server-requirements/`,
};
---

<Layout
  title="How Much RAM and CPU Does a FiveM Server Need?"
  description="FiveM server requirements in plain numbers: CPU, RAM and storage by player count and framework, plus the asset mistakes that leave players stuck loading."
  ogType="article"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "FiveM Server Requirements", path: "/guides/fivem-server-requirements/" }]}
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <section class="section border-b border-[var(--edge)]">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="instrument text-fog">Guide · FiveM</p>
      <h1 class="display-xl mt-4 max-w-3xl">How Much RAM and CPU Does a FiveM Server Need?</h1>
      <p class="lead text-fog mt-6 max-w-xl">
        Short answer: CPU speed matters more than core count, 6 to 8 GB of RAM
        carries most roleplay servers, and your assets decide how long players
        wait on the loading screen. The details are below. These are rules of
        thumb from running these servers, not hard limits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="shell max-w-3xl space-y-12">
      <div>
        <h2 class="title">CPU: One Fast Core Beats Many Slow Ones</h2>
        <p class="small text-fog mt-4 max-w-prose">
          The FiveM server runs most of its scripting on one main thread. A
          resource that does heavy work on that thread slows everything else,
          which shows up as the "server thread hitch warning" lines in your
          console. Extra cores help with networking and background work, but a
          fast single core is what keeps hitches away. When you compare hosts,
          ask what CPU model your cores come from and whether they are
          dedicated or shared. Shared cores are where a quiet night turns into
          a laggy one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="title">RAM by Server Type</h2>
        <p class="small text-fog mt-4 max-w-prose">
          Use these as a starting point, then watch real usage in txAdmin for a
          week and adjust.
        </p>
        <ul class="small text-fog mt-4 max-w-prose list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li><span class="text-paper">2 to 4 GB:</span> A small friends server, a test server, or a light freeroam setup with few resources.</li>
          <li><span class="text-paper">6 to 8 GB:</span> A full QBCore or ESX roleplay server with a normal resource list and a regular player base.</li>
          <li><span class="text-paper">10 GB and up:</span> A very heavy resource list, many custom scripts, or a busy server that runs a lot of players at peak.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small text-fog mt-4 max-w-prose">
          Your database is separate from this. Run MySQL or MariaDB with
          oxmysql for your framework, and keep the connection string in
          your server.cfg out of anything public.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="title">Storage and Assets: Where Loading Screens Get Stuck</h2>
        <p class="small text-fog mt-4 max-w-prose">
          Disk space matters less than what sits in it. Every vehicle pack,
          clothing pack and map you add is something each player downloads and
          streams before they can spawn. A server with a few gigabytes of
          assets can leave new players on "Awaiting scripts" for minutes,
          and some quit before it finishes. The usual causes are single texture
          files of 32 to 64 MB, which FiveM warns about in the console as
          oversized assets. Compress or replace those files, and drop packs
          you do not use. Fast NVMe storage speeds up restarts and loading on
          the server side, but it cannot shrink what players have to download.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="title">Slots Are a License Limit, Not a Hardware One</h2>
        <p class="small text-fog mt-4 max-w-prose">
          The maximum number of players your server can accept comes from your
          Cfx license. Your CPU and RAM decide how many of those players the
          server carries without hitching. Lumix does not meter slots on FiveM
          plans, so size the plan to the load, not to a slot count.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="title">Which Lumix FiveM Plan Fits</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="small w-full min-w-[32rem] text-left text-fog">
            <thead class="text-paper">
              <tr class="border-b border-[var(--edge)]">
                <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Plan</th>
                <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">vCores</th>
                <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">RAM</th>
                <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">NVMe</th>
                <th class="py-3 font-medium">From</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {plans.map((p) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-[var(--edge)]">
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-paper">{p.name}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 tabular">{p.specs.vcores}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 tabular">{p.specs.ramGb} GB</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 tabular">{p.specs.storageGb} GB</td>
                  <td class="py-3 tabular">{price(p.pricing.monthly)}/mo</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small text-fog mt-6 max-w-prose">
          Start with the plan that matches your server type above. You can
          upgrade from the client area at any time and pay the difference.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
          <a
            href="/games/fivem/"
            class="chamfer inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-red px-7 py-4 font-medium text-ink transition-colors duration-150 hover:bg-red-pressed"
          >
            See FiveM Server Hosting Plans
            <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
          <a
            href="/tools/fivem-server-cfg/"
            class="chamfer inline-flex items-center gap-2 border border-[var(--edge-strong)] px-7 py-4 font-medium text-paper transition-colors duration-150 hover:border-red hover:text-red"
          >
            Build Your server.cfg
            <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
